=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
A thorough inspection of every OSPF-speaking device in the Kath4 campus network (router1, router7, router8, router10, router11, router16, router17, mswitch0, sshswitch — nine routers in Area 0) found the domain completely healthy and stable. No OSPF adjacency flapping is occurring, and there is no evidence that any has ever occurred in this lab instance.

Specific checks performed and their results:

**Adjacency state**: All 12 OSPF adjacencies across the topology are Full. Up-times grew monotonically from ~1 minute to ~6 minutes between my first and final snapshots — no adjacency reset occurred during the investigation.

**Timers**: Every interface on every device reports Hello 10 s / Dead 40 s / Retransmit 5. There are no mismatches anywhere.

**LSA database**: Sequence numbers are all in the range 0x80000001–0x8000000b (at most 11 increments since start), ruling out any history of rapid LSA churn or repeated SPF recalculations. SPF execution counts (12–15 runs) are consistent with normal initial convergence.

**Bond interfaces** (router16 ↔ mswitch0 via bond0/br0; router17 ↔ sshswitch via bond0/br_v1, both using balance-rr mode 0): MII Status is UP on both slave interfaces with Link Failure Count = 0. These links have not dropped once.

**Data plane**: All pings from router7 to each of its four OSPF peers return 0% packet loss with sub-millisecond RTT.

**Startup configs vs. running configs**: Identical on every device. No injected fault, no kill/restart script, no cron job was found.

**Root cause**: There is no fault. The NOC report of "bouncing adjacencies" does not correspond to any real condition in the network. The OSPF area is fully converged and has been since initial lab startup.

**Corrective action**: None required. The ticket should be closed as a false alarm.
=== END ===